const copy = {
  'circuit-de-france-1942': { year:'1942', label:'Racing and society · Occupied France', title:'The Tour the occupation wanted', intro:'The Circuit de France was launched as a six-stage substitute race in occupied France. On a map it resembled the Tour. In practice, it showed how much was missing.', alt:'Illustration of a peloton on a worn European road during the shortages of the 1940s', caption:'A contemporary interpretation of a peloton under the scarce conditions of the 1940s. AI-generated illustration — not a documentary image of the Circuit de France. Illustration: The Evolution of Bikes.', sections:[['A Tour de France without the Tour','The Tour de France was not held during the German occupation. In 1942, sports journalist Jean Leulliot and the newspaper La France socialiste instead attempted to stage the Circuit de France. It began on 28 September and ended on 4 October after six stages through both the occupied and the so-called free zone.'],['A race with political baggage','The newspaper behind the race collaborated with the occupying power, and the event could be presented as evidence of order and normality. That did not make every rider a political actor. But the organiser, the permissions and the coverage cannot be separated from the circumstances in which the race took place.'],['A calendar without fuel or free movement','Transport, petrol, spare parts and food were rationed. Borders and checkpoints made planning difficult, and several leading riders and teams stayed away. The Circuit de France was therefore neither the Tour under a new name nor a normal international stage race.'],['François Neuville won','Belgian François Neuville finished as overall winner. The result is clear, but the race’s main significance lies elsewhere. This was its only edition; plans for a repeat in 1943 came to nothing.'],['What the absence revealed','The Circuit de France showed that a route and a field do not by themselves create a Tour de France. The race also required a newspaper, an audience, supplies, freedom of movement and a degree of public trust. In 1942, each existed only in a limited form. It was not enough.']], sources:[['Hugh Dauncey: French Cycling — A Social and Cultural History','https://tile.loc.gov/storage-services/master/gdc/gdcebookspublic/20/16/44/94/98/2016449498/2016449498.pdf'],['Tour de France: 1940, the Tour that was not held','https://www.letour.fr/en/news/2020/1940-the-tour-that-wasnt-4-10/1283547']] },
  'gino-bartali-kurer-under-krigen': { year:'1943–1944', label:'The people behind cycling · Second World War', title:"Bartali's training rides had another purpose", intro:'Gino Bartali was known for training over long distances. During the German occupation of Italy, that familiar habit made him useful as a courier for a rescue network.', alt:'Gino Bartali after the Tour of Lombardy in 1936', caption:'Gino Bartali in 1936, before the war interrupted the major international races. Photograph: Agence Meurisse/Bibliothèque nationale de France/Wikimedia Commons, public domain.', sections:[['A famous man on familiar roads','After the German occupation of northern and central Italy in September 1943, Bartali worked as a courier for a network around Cardinal Elia Dalla Costa and Rabbi Nathan Cassuto. His long journeys between places including Florence and Assisi looked like what the public already knew him for: a professional rider in training.'],['Documents between cities','Yad Vashem’s investigation documents that Bartali carried false papers and other material between members of the network. Sources describe different hiding places on the bicycle, so the precise location should not be made the story’s single certain detail. His role as a courier is firmly established.'],['The bicycle as an explanation','When stopped, Bartali could point to his training and his famous name. Witness accounts say that he asked officials not to touch the bicycle because its adjustment was vital to his sport. The explanation worked precisely because a professional racing bicycle really was a carefully fitted working tool.'],['Part of a larger network','Bartali did not act alone. Priests, Jewish helpers, forgers, families and couriers each carried out part of the work. The often-repeated figure of more than 800 people saved should therefore not simply be credited to Bartali personally. His contribution was real; it was also collective.'],['A story he did not sell','After the war, Bartali said little publicly about this work. In 2013, Yad Vashem recognised him as Righteous Among the Nations. The recognition rests on testimony and his documented role, not on a need to turn one famous rider into the network’s only hero.']], sources:[['Yad Vashem: Gino Bartali, Righteous Among the Nations','https://wwv.yadvashem.org/yv/en/exhibitions/righteous-sportsmen/bartali.asp'],['United States Holocaust Memorial Museum: Gino Bartali','https://encyclopedia.ushmm.org/content/en/article/gino-bartali']] },
  'fausto-coppi-fra-krigsfange-til-comeback': { year:'1943–1949', label:'Riders of the period · War and return', title:'From prisoner of war to Giro–Tour winner', intro:'Fausto Coppi had already won the Giro when he was sent to North Africa and captured. When the races returned, the second part of his career began as well.', alt:'Fausto Coppi at the summit of the Ghisallo during the 1957 Tour of Lombardy', caption:'Fausto Coppi on the Ghisallo in 1957. The image is later than the events described, but shows the rider who came to define post-war cycle racing. Photograph: unknown/Wikimedia Commons, public domain.', sections:[['A Giro winner before call-up','Coppi won the 1940 Giro d’Italia at the age of 20. A few days after the race ended, Italy entered the war. His breakthrough therefore did not begin a normal run of international seasons, but several years of military service, interrupted racing and uncertainty.'],['To North Africa','In 1943, Coppi was sent to Tunisia with the Italian army. British forces captured him in April near Mateur and Medjez el-Bab. In captivity he was first a soldier and prisoner of war, not the public champion Italian newspapers had made him.'],['Back through Italy','Coppi was later brought back to Italy with the Allied advance. A surviving letter from November 1944 places him in Caserta. After the war he could race again, but the calendar, teams and supplies had to be reassembled just as the riders did.'],['The victories returned quickly','In 1946, Coppi won Milan–San Remo after a long solo ride. He won the Giro in 1947 and, in 1949, both the Giro d’Italia and Tour de France. He became the first rider to win the two major stage races in the same season.'],['Not an unbroken line of talent','Coppi’s results can look like a straight line from young Giro winner to the post-war Campionissimo. The war cut a large gap through that line. His return is therefore not only a story of talent and modern training, but of a professional career that had to begin again.']], sources:[['Giro d’Italia: historical archive 1933–1947','https://archivio.giroditalia.it/eng/editions/1933-1947/'],['Federazione fra le Società Filateliche Italiane: Coppi, war and surviving correspondence','https://expo.fsfi.it/vastophil2023/exhibits/06PoloWHY2MUdW.pdf']] },
  'jean-robic-vender-touren-1947': { year:'1947', label:'Races and great moments · Tour de France', title:'Robic took yellow on the final day', intro:'Jean Robic had not worn the yellow jersey for a single day. When the final stage began, he was third. On the road to Paris, he overturned the entire classification.', alt:'Jean Robic photographed after winning the 1947 Tour de France', caption:'Jean Robic after his victory in the 1947 Tour de France. Photograph: Anefo/Nationaal Archief, CC0.', sections:[['The Tour returns','The Tour de France was held again in 1947 after a seven-year absence. Pierre Brambilla led before the final stage from Caen to Paris. René Vietto was second and Jean Robic began the day in third. Normally, the road to Paris was not where the overall race was overturned.'],['The attack after Rouen','Robic attacked after Rouen and Édouard Fachleitner joined him. Brambilla could not close the gap. By the time the group reached the Parc des Princes, Robic had gained more than the time he needed and moved into first place in the final classification.'],['Yellow on the podium, not on the road','He thus won the Tour without previously wearing the yellow jersey during the race. It is a peculiar detail, but it should not obscure the achievement: the overall lead was attacked and lost on the final day.'],['An agreement on the road','Later accounts describe agreements between Robic and Fachleitner involving assistance and prize money. The exact words and sums vary. Their cooperation in the attack belongs to the course of the race; every detail of the verbal bargain cannot be treated as a contemporary transcript.'],['The lead bottle came later','Robic is also linked to a story about carrying a heavy bottle filled with lead on mountain descents. That episode is normally associated with the 1953 Tour and does not explain his 1947 victory. He won this day by attacking while the race was still open.']], sources:[['Tour de France: Robic turns the 1947 Tour on its head','https://www.letour.fr/en/news/2024/finish-with-a-capital-f-parc-des-princes-the-home-of-cycling-ii-iv/1316959']] },
  'campagnolo-gran-sport-parallelogram': { year:'1951', label:'Technology · Gear shifting', title:'When gear shifting finally became modern', intro:'Gran Sport made shifting a movement of the derailleur rather than the rear wheel. The modern principle was not new, but Campagnolo placed it at the centre of the Italian racing bicycle.', alt:'Early-1950s Giamé racing bicycle with period gear equipment', caption:'The collection’s early-1950s Giamé shows the kind of light steel bicycle for which the new derailleurs were developed. This bicycle is not documented as having Campagnolo Gran Sport.', sections:[['Before the wheel could stay in place','Cambio Corsa and the Paris–Roubaix gear allowed the rider to shift while moving, but the rear axle still had to be released and the wheel moved in the frame. It demanded both balance and mechanical sensitivity.'],['The parallelogram','Gran Sport moved the chain guide sideways through an articulated parallelogram. A spring kept the chain tensioned while two pulley wheels guided it between sprockets. The rear wheel could now remain securely fixed.'],['Not the first derailleur','Campagnolo did not invent the parallelogram principle. Other manufacturers had already developed related solutions. Its importance lay in a robust, prestigious model closely associated with the fine Italian racing bicycle.'],['A lever on the down tube','Shifting was still not indexed. The rider reached for a friction lever on the down tube and found the quiet chain position by feel. It was modern beside the earlier rod system, but still demanded practice.'],['A form that endured','Gran Sport pointed towards decades of rear derailleurs. Materials, sprocket counts and controls changed, but the basic parallelogram idea remained.']], sources:[['Campagnolo: company history','https://www.campagnolo.com/us-en/company/history/'],['The Evolution of Bikes: Cambio Corsa','https://teob.dk/en/components/campagnolo-cambio-corsa/']] },
  'roger-walkowiak-tour-1956': { year:'1956', label:'Races and great moments · Tour de France', title:'The Tour winner nobody expected', intro:'Roger Walkowiak won no stage in the 1956 Tour. He won the race because the favourites first underestimated his breakaway and later could not drop him.', alt:'Tour de France riders receiving musettes during the 1958 race', caption:'A feed zone during the 1958 Tour de France. The image shows everyday race life shortly after Walkowiak’s victory, but neither Walkowiak nor the 1956 Tour. Photograph: Nationaal Archief, no known restrictions.', sections:[['The regional team','Walkowiak was not selected for the French national team. He rode for Nord-Est–Centre and began without the attention attached to the race’s greatest names.'],['The breakaway was given room','On stage seven, he joined a large break that the favourites allowed to gain too much time. He took the yellow jersey, lost it again and still remained close in the classification.'],['The decisive defence','In the Alps he showed that the advantage was not merely a gift. He followed the strongest riders, regained the lead four days before Paris and denied his rivals the chance to correct their early misjudgement.'],['No stage victory','Winning without a stage made the story difficult to sell as conventional heroism. The phrase “à la Walko” was later used for a surprising or supposedly lucky victory.'],['More than luck','A breakaway can provide time. It cannot by itself defend that time over mountains and through a three-week race. Walkowiak’s victory was unexpected, but not accidental.']], sources:[['Tour de France: One last breakaway for Walko','https://www.letour.fr/en/news/2017/one-last-breakaway-for-walko/1263971']] },
  'charly-gaul-monte-bondone-1956': { year:'1956', label:'Races and great moments · Giro d’Italia', title:'When the snow broke the Giro peloton', intro:'Rain turned to snow on the 242-kilometre stage from Merano to Monte Bondone. Charly Gaul attacked early and finished the day as stage winner and new man in pink.', alt:'Charly Gaul at the team presentation before the 1958 Tour de France', caption:'Charly Gaul stands in the centre at the team presentation before the 1958 Tour de France. The photograph was taken two years after Monte Bondone. Photograph: Harry Pot/Anefo, CC0.', sections:[['Four passes before the finish','The stage crossed Costalunga, Rolle, Gobbera and Brocon before finishing on Monte Bondone. Cold, rain and wind turned the opening hours into an elimination race.'],['Gaul attacks','Charly Gaul began the day in eleventh place, more than sixteen minutes behind Pasquale Fornara. He attacked as the weather worsened and riders disappeared from the field.'],['Snow on Bondone','On the final climb, rain became a snowstorm. Fornara’s directeur sportif withdrew him out of concern for his health. Gaul continued through the cold and won the stage by almost eight minutes.'],['Close to hypothermia','At the finish, Gaul was so badly affected that helpers had to get him off the bicycle and into warmth. The hypothermia should not be treated as decoration: the stage showed how little safety margin separated racing from serious harm.'],['From eleventh to victory','The stage gave Gaul the pink jersey, which he kept to Milan. Bondone endured as one of the Giro’s clearest images of a person pushed to the limit.']], sources:[['Giro d’Italia: Monte Bondone 1956','https://www.giroditalia.it/en/monte-bondone/'],['Giro d’Italia: memories from Monte Bondone','https://www.giroditalia.it/news/ricordi-dal-monte-bondone-1956-luomo-portato-ai-suoi-limiti/']] },
  'tour-start-amsterdam-1954': { year:'1954', label:'Society and the times · Tour de France', title:'When the Tour left France', intro:'The Tour had crossed national borders for years, but its start belonged to France. In 1954, the race began in another country for the first time.', alt:'The Tour de France peloton passing spectators in the Netherlands in 1954', caption:'The peloton on Zandvoortselaan during the Tour’s first Grand Départ outside France in 1954. Photograph: Nationaal Archief, no known restrictions.', sections:[['Amsterdam before Paris','On 8 July 1954, the Tour de France began in Amsterdam. It was the race’s first Grand Départ outside France, although the route had crossed national borders many times before.'],['A Dutch opening day','The first stage ran to Brasschaat in Belgium. Wout Wagtmans won and became the first Dutch rider in yellow after a Tour start on home soil.'],['A European race','Post-war Europe still carried the ruptures of the conflict. Amsterdam cannot simply be called a reconciliation project, but the start showed a race increasingly presenting itself as a shared European event.'],['Cities became participants','A foreign start gave the host city spectators, press coverage and international attention. The model later became a permanent part of the Tour’s geographical and economic expansion.'],['A new beginning','Amsterdam did not change the bicycle. The city changed the idea of where the Tour could begin.']], sources:[['Tour de France: Grands Départs','https://www.letour.fr/en/the-race/grands-departs']] },
  'fausto-coppi-la-bomba': { year:'1952', label:'Nutrition, training and science · Doping history', title:'When “necessary” meant almost always', intro:'Fausto Coppi’s answer about “la bomba” became one of cycling’s most frequently repeated doping remarks. It belongs to an age when use was known but effective controls barely existed.', alt:'Fausto Coppi during the 1957 Tour of Lombardy', caption:'Fausto Coppi on the Ghisallo in 1957. The photograph is later than the interview. Photograph: unknown/Wikimedia Commons; free-use status recorded in the project image archive.', sections:[['A direct question','In a 1952 RAI interview, Coppi was asked whether riders took “la bomba”, the peloton’s term for amphetamine-based stimulants. He answered without the distance created by later rules.'],['Almost always','Asked when it was necessary, he replied in the familiar version: practically all the time. The remark survives through later RAI broadcasts and should be tied to this particular interview.'],['A tool of the trade','Amphetamine could suppress tiredness and hunger and create a feeling of surplus energy. That was precisely what made it dangerous: the body’s warnings grew weaker while the load continued.'],['Before controls','There was still no effective international testing system. This does not mean that use was harmless or universally accepted, but that the boundary between medicine, stimulant and cheating was handled far more loosely.'],['A remark with another side','Coppi’s dry answer can sound amusing. Historically, it matters more as a rare open window into a working culture in which riders pushed the body without the safeguards later rules attempted to create.']], sources:[['RAI: Fausto e la bomba','https://www.raiplay.it/video/2019/07/Coppi100-Fausto-e-la-bomba-592bfdb3-8643-4dd3-8e95-bb0952d21807.html']] },
  'moser-timerekord-1984': { year:'1984', label:'Technology · Track cycling', title:'When Moser rode 51.151 kilometres in an hour', intro:'Across four January days in Mexico City, Francesco Moser moved the hour record twice. Disc wheels, altitude and a new approach to drag turned the bicycle into a laboratory.', alt:'Illustration of a track rider on an aerodynamic disc-wheel bicycle in a 1984 velodrome', caption:'Period reconstruction of Moser’s record attempt in Mexico City. AI-generated illustration — not a historical photograph. Illustration: The Evolution of Bikes.', sections:[['The black line','On 19 January 1984, Francesco Moser covered 50.808 kilometres in Mexico City. Four days later, he returned and reached 51.151. Eddy Merckx’s 1972 mark had not merely been beaten; it had entered a new technical age.'],['Altitude and the body','Mexico City’s thin air reduced drag but also supplied less oxygen. Moser and physician Francesco Conconi prepared with testing, training and a systematic focus on sixty minutes. Rider, physiology, track and equipment worked together.'],['A bicycle that looked wrong','Solid disc wheels and a compact position broke with the familiar track bicycle. The frame was still steel, but the air around it now dictated the shape. Every projection and angle became part of the calculation.'],['More than two wheels','The discs became the visible symbol, but they did not ride alone. Moser was an exceptional rider, the track was at altitude and the preparation served one task. Technology mattered because the whole attempt was organised around it.'],['After the record','The UCI later changed its hour-record rules and separated equipment categories in the historical lists. January 1984 still showed a broad audience that aerodynamics could determine how far a human travelled in an hour.']], sources:[['UCI: Historical hour records','https://assets.ctfassets.net/761l7gh5x5an/052qz35QNDHZmP2Qvtx77/ef7344311538ddc6399cdcd8e4e8093f/20240816_Historique_des_records_ME.pdf'],['Cyclingnews: Francesco Moser’s hour record','https://www.cyclingnews.com/news/gallery-francesco-mosers-hour-record/']] },
  'look-klikpedal-hinault-1985': { year:'1984–1985', label:'Technology · Tour de France', title:'When the toe strap gave way to a click', intro:'A foot in a toe clip was secure, but it also had to get out. LOOK borrowed the release from a ski binding and changed the connection between rider and bicycle.', alt:'Bernard Hinault wearing the world champion’s jersey in 1982', caption:'Bernard Hinault in 1982, before winning his fifth Tour on LOOK’s new pedal system. Photograph: Marcel Antonisse/Anefo, CC0.', sections:[['The strap across the foot','A racing shoe sat in a metal toe clip and under a leather strap. It held the foot firmly, but releasing it required a hand movement. At a sudden stop or in a crash, that could be a problem.'],['The ski binding on a bicycle','LOOK already understood releasable ski bindings. The PP65 used a cleat beneath the shoe and a sprung lock. The rider pressed down to engage and turned the heel out to release.'],['Hinault as the test rider','Bernard Hinault used the system with La Vie Claire. He crashed heavily on the 1985 Tour stage to Saint-Étienne, but the pedals did not become the weak link in the story. He continued and won a fifth Tour.'],['Not an overnight change','The peloton did not discard every toe clip in a day. Riders had to trust the spring, shoes had to fit the cleat and mechanics had to learn the system. Victory gave it visibility no advertisement could buy.'],['A small click with a lasting effect','Clipless pedals made entry and release quicker through one defined movement. Shapes and materials changed, but the principle stayed. One of the bicycle’s oldest connections had become new.']], sources:[['LOOK: 40 years of Science Meets Art','https://www.lookcycle.com/us-en/inside/news/40-years-of-science-meets-art'],['LOOK heritage','https://www.lookcycle.com/ja-en/inside/about-look-heritage'],['Wikimedia Commons: Bernard Hinault, 1982','https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bernard_Hinault_(1982).jpg']] },
  'gavia-sneetapen-1988': { year:'1988', label:'Races and great moments · Giro d’Italia', title:'When the summer peloton rode into snow', intro:'The stage began in rain on 5 June. On the Passo di Gavia the road was white, hands were numb and the line between continuing and stopping almost disappeared.', alt:'Illustration of exhausted racing cyclists in heavy snow on an Italian mountain pass in 1988', caption:'Period reconstruction of the 1988 Gavia snow stage. AI-generated illustration — not a historical photograph. Illustration: The Evolution of Bikes.', sections:[['A short stage on paper','The 120 kilometres from Chiesa in Valmalenco to Bormio looked modest. Rain in the valley turned to snow above. The Gavia was not yet the broad, controlled mountain road a modern race would prefer.'],['Van der Velde first over','Johan van der Velde reached the summit first. On the descent he sought shelter in a camper and eventually lost 47 minutes. This was not an ordinary tactical collapse. Cold made clothing, braking and judgement part of one problem.'],['Breukink won, Hampsten took pink','Erik Breukink won in Bormio. Andy Hampsten arrived just behind and took the pink jersey by 15 seconds. He kept it to the finish and became the Giro’s first American winner.'],['The helpers beside the road','Extra clothing, warm drinks and hands from team staff could make the difference, but support was uneven on a mountain with riders scattered across it. Soigneurs and mechanics became as important as gearing.'],['The image that remained','Gavia 1988 is remembered as heroic, but the cold was not scenery. It was a genuine danger. The stage endured because it showed both the riders’ resolve and the limit of what a race can ask.']], sources:[['Giro d’Italia: Gavia','https://www.giroditalia.it/en/gavia/']] },
  'lemond-fignon-otte-sekunder-1989': { year:'1989', label:'Races and great moments · Tour de France', title:'Eight seconds on the Champs-Élysées', intro:'Laurent Fignon began the Tour’s final day with 50 seconds in hand. After 24.5 kilometres against the clock, Greg LeMond had won the race by eight.', alt:'Greg LeMond during the final time trial of the 1989 Tour de France', caption:'Greg LeMond during the decisive time trial to the Champs-Élysées in 1989. Photograph: Benjamin Werner, CC BY 2.0.', sections:[['A race still open','The 1989 Tour had changed hands several times. Before the last stage, Laurent Fignon led by 50 seconds. Paris would normally have been a procession. This time, a 24.5-kilometre time trial began in Versailles.'],['LeMond made himself narrow','Greg LeMond used aero bars, an aerodynamic helmet and a compact position. The equipment made the difference visible, but the ride also came from pacing, training and a rider seriously wounded in a hunting accident two years earlier.'],['Fifty-eight seconds on the road','LeMond completed the stage in 26 minutes 57 seconds. Fignon took 58 seconds longer. A 50-second deficit became an eight-second overall victory, the smallest margin in Tour history.'],['Fignon was not old-fashioned','The story becomes too simple if Fignon is reduced to the man without the right handlebar. He had won the Tour twice and ridden a strong race. The time trial was a sum of form, position, choices and seconds across the course.'],['When aerodynamics acquired a face','Eight seconds gave the development a story everyone understood. After Paris, no leading team could treat a rider’s frontal area as a detail. The time-trial bicycle had become a tool capable of moving the Tour itself.']], sources:[['Tour de France: the 1989 finish','https://www.letour.fr/en/news/2024/finish-with-a-capital-f-champs-elysees-welcome-to-sprinter-heaven-iv-iv/1316968'],['ProCyclingStats: LeMond–Fignon final time trial','https://www.procyclingstats.com/key-event/fignon-lemond-in-final-time-trial-tour-1989'],['Wikimedia Commons: Greg LeMond, stage 21 TT','https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Greg_LeMond_1989_Tour_de_France_stage_21_TT.jpg']] },
  'soigneuren-og-maden-i-1970erne': { year:'The 1970s', label:'Nutrition, training and science · The people behind cycling', title:'Steak at five and coffee with cognac', intro:'Before gels and precise carbohydrate plans, the soigneur packed sandwiches, fruit and tarts, filled the bottles and tried to keep riders eating through a long working day.', alt:'The Molteni team with Eddy Merckx during the team time trial at the 1971 Tour de France', caption:'The Molteni team during the team time trial at the 1971 Tour de France. The photograph shows the period’s team organisation, not a feed zone. Photograph: Anefo · CC BY-SA 3.0 NL.', sections:[['Before the riders came down to breakfast','The soigneur began work before the riders. Bottles had to be filled, small sandwiches prepared and the day’s musettes distributed. The role covered much more than massage. Soigneurs also handled clothing, drinks, food and much of the practical order around the team.'],['Steak and rice before sunrise','Breakfast could be heavy by modern standards. Steak and rice were regarded as reliable fuel and eaten several hours before the start. In 1977, Eddy Merckx said that for years he had eaten steak with rice at five or six in the morning and had reached the point where he could no longer face it. André Dierickx remembered split-stage days when riders might be served steak three times.'],['The bag at the roadside','In the feed zone, soigneurs held cloth bags at shoulder height. The rider had to find the right person, catch the strap without pulling the bag into the wheel and quickly move the contents into jersey pockets. A musette might contain small sandwiches, fruit, sugar, tarts or other food that could be eaten while riding. Contents varied between teams, races and riders; there was no single recipe for the whole peloton.'],['Sweet coffee and something stronger','Strong sweet coffee was common in cycling provisions, and accounts from the period also mention small mixtures of coffee and cognac. Alcohol was neither a precise fuelling plan nor a sound treatment for cramp. It belonged to a working culture in which experience, habit and anecdote carried more weight than modern sports nutrition. Cola was also used as a quick source of sugar, often after some of the carbonation had been released.'],['When the musette was missed','A dropped musette could be costly, especially when the next opportunity for food lay far ahead. The rider was not necessarily left without help; under certain conditions, rules allowed bottles and food from the team car. Yet too little food during a long race could still bring the feared fringale, the hunger crisis in which pace, concentration and judgement disappeared almost at once.'],['Between craft and science','The 1970s were not a decade without knowledge. Teams learned from experience, and some worked more systematically than others. Nutrition, however, was rarely planned in grams of carbohydrate per hour. The soigneur’s essential task was more ordinary: know the riders, prepare the food and make sure it was actually eaten. That required no laboratory. It required an alarm clock, a knife and a considerable amount of white bread.']], sources:[['KOERS Museum: Jean-Pierre Monseré – steak as a source of strength','https://servicekoers.be/verhalen/jean-pierre-monsere-biefstuk'],['KOERS Museum: La Grande Bouffe – feeding during the Tour','https://servicekoers.be/verhalen/bevoorrading-tijdens-de-tour'],['KOERS Museum: Briek Schotte – bread in the musette','https://servicekoers.be/verhalen/briek-schotte-brood'],['Wikimedia Commons: Molteni team, Tour de France 1971','https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Molteni,_Team_time_trial_prologue,_1971_Tour_de_France.jpg']] },
  'boonen-paris-roubaix-2009': { year:'2009', label:'Races and great moments · Paris–Roubaix', title:'When the Roubaix favourites disappeared one by one', intro:'Six riders reached Carrefour de l’Arbre together. Minutes later several challengers were gone and Tom Boonen had a clear road to his third victory.', alt:'Tom Boonen winning Paris–Roubaix 2009 in the Roubaix velodrome', caption:'Tom Boonen finishes Paris–Roubaix 2009 in the velodrome. Photograph: Thomas Ducroquet/Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0.', sections:[['Six riders remained','After Mons-en-Pévèle, Boonen was in a strong group with Pozzato, Hushovd, Flecha, Hoste and Vansummeren. As a twice winner, he was the rider nobody wanted to take to the velodrome.'],['Flecha makes his move','Flecha entered Carrefour de l’Arbre believing he could decide the race. He crashed in the first bend, damaged his bicycle and lost his chance within seconds. He later remembered 2009 as the year he came closest.'],['Hushovd hits the barriers','Boonen and Hushovd escaped, but the Norwegian crashed into the barriers on the following cobbled sector. Boonen had not caused either fall. His advantage was that he remained upright, carried speed and knew the road home.'],['Alone to Roubaix','Boonen rode solo and won 47 seconds ahead of Pozzato, with Hushovd third. It was his third Roubaix victory after 2005 and 2008.'],['The simple arithmetic of cobbles','Strength alone did not settle the race. Position, line choice, equipment and staying upright sorted a group that had looked evenly matched only kilometres earlier. Roubaix rarely needs help creating disorder.']], sources:[['Paris–Roubaix: Juan Antonio Flecha on 2009','https://www.paris-roubaix.fr/en/news/2026/juan-antonio-flecha-paris-roubaix-picks-its-winners-and-sometimes-it-is-unfair-in-its-choices/4769'],['Wikimedia Commons: Boonen, Roubaix 2009','https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boonen_Roubaix_2009_1_(cropped).jpg']] },
  'operacion-puerto-2006': { year:'2006', label:'Society and its time · Doping', title:'The blood bags that reached the Tour before the start', intro:'The Tour peloton was ready in Strasbourg when names from a Spanish police investigation began to have practical consequences. Ivan Basso and Jan Ullrich never started.', alt:'Jan Ullrich during the 2003 Tour de France time trial', caption:'Jan Ullrich in the 2003 Tour. Three years later his team withdrew him before the start following information from Operación Puerto. Photograph: BerndtF, CC BY-SA 3.0.', sections:[['An investigation in Madrid','Spain’s Guardia Civil investigated doctor Eufemiano Fuentes and a network involving blood transfusions and doping products. A May 2006 operation seized documents, equipment, blood and plasma. The UCI later put the total at 211 bags.'],['The start list changed','Days before the Tour began in Strasbourg, the case was linked to numerous riders. Favourites Ivan Basso and Jan Ullrich were removed by their teams. Astana-Würth no longer had enough riders to start.'],['Codes, dog names and DNA','Fuentes’ records used code names. Later proceedings and DNA comparisons linked riders including Basso and Ullrich to blood bags. Basso admitted having blood extracted for an intended later transfusion.'],['Different systems','Anti-doping rules, Spanish criminal law and access to police evidence did not work in the same way. WADA and the UCI spent years trying to obtain and use the material in sporting cases.'],['Before the first stage','Operación Puerto changed the Tour without a kilometre being raced. The last-minute exclusions did not solve cycling’s problem. They showed how organised it had become and how awkwardly evidence moved between police, courts and sport.']], sources:[['UCI: Operation Puerto blood bags','https://www.uci.org/pressrelease/uci-statement-on-operation-puerto-blood-bags-177532-a341/54bza669eavkjoKa3w3M3O'],['CAS: Valverde and Operación Puerto','https://www.wada-ama.org/sites/default/files/resources/files/cas_2009_a_1879_valverde_v_coni_en_0.pdf']] },
  'bjarne-riis-team-csc': { year:'2001–2009', label:'The people behind cycling · Team management', title:'When Bjarne Riis built Team CSC', intro:'Bjarne Riis took over a Danish team with uncertain finances. During the decade it became an international organisation that won the Tour with Carlos Sastre.', alt:'Carlos Sastre in the yellow jersey on the Champs-Élysées in 2008', caption:'Carlos Sastre completes the 2008 Tour in yellow for Team CSC–Saxo Bank. Photograph: Bjarte Hetland, CC BY 3.0.', sections:[['A team changes hands','After retiring, Riis entered the management of the Danish team previously known as Home–Jack & Jones and Memory Card–Jack & Jones. CSC sponsorship from 2001 gave it an international framework, but a name alone did not produce results.'],['Roles and preparation','Riis assembled riders, directors, coaches and technicians around a more fixed working method. Training data, equipment testing and carefully planned programmes became routine. These trends belonged to the whole sport, but Team CSC made them part of its public identity.'],['Team spirit away from the bicycle','Survival camps led by former special-forces soldier B.S. Christiansen became famous. They were not cycling training, but tests of communication, responsibility and behaviour under pressure. Some riders found value in them; others were less enthusiastic.'],['Victory on Alpe d’Huez','In 2008 Carlos Sastre attacked on Alpe d’Huez, took yellow and defended it in the time trial. The Tour victory became the clearest sporting high point of Riis’s team.'],['A complicated legacy','In 2007 Riis admitted using EPO as a rider in the 1990s. That changed how his leadership was viewed. The CSC story contains both skilled organisation and the distrust surrounding professional cycling in this period.']], sources:[['Tour de France: Carlos Sastre','https://www.letour.fr/en/rider/carlos-sastre'],['UCI team archive','https://www.uci.org/']] },
  'carbon-og-68-kilo': { year:'2000–2009', label:'Bicycles and technical change', title:'When the carbon bicycle reached the weight limit', intro:'Carbon allowed freer frame shapes and lower weight. The UCI’s 6.8-kilogram minimum meant that the lightest bicycle could require ballast before it was legal to race.', alt:'The Tour de France peloton climbing Col d’Allos in 2000', caption:'The Tour peloton on Col d’Allos in 2000, when aluminium and carbon still shared the road. Photograph: Lazz06, CC BY-SA 4.0.', sections:[['Several materials on one start line','At the start of the decade professionals still raced aluminium, carbon, titanium and a few steel frames. Carbon was not new, but production improved and larger monocoque sections offered possibilities beyond joined metal tubes.'],['Shape moved','Fibres could follow loads and tubes no longer had to be round. Bottom brackets, head tubes and rear triangles grew. Aerodynamics and stiffness became visible sales terms, but sound design still required control of weight, impact strength and handling.'],['The 6.8-kilogram floor','UCI rules set a 6.8-kilogram minimum for the complete bicycle. As top machines approached it, mechanics had to verify every setup and sometimes add ballast. Safety and consistent inspection took precedence over the lowest possible number.'],['Gram hunting with consequences','Titanium bolts, light saddles, thin paint and selected wheels saved grams. Mechanics also had to ensure the bicycle survived braking, cobbles and three weeks of racing. The lightest solution was not always the fastest or most reliable.'],['The new normal','By the end of the 2000s carbon dominated top-level bicycles. Craft did not disappear. Much of it moved from the brazing torch to moulds, fibre orientation, test laboratories and a scale beside the team bus.']], sources:[['UCI: Equipment and technical regulations','https://www.uci.org/equipment/bh2JJzw1eB0n876rX2iB1']] },
  'froome-finestre-2018': { year:'2018', label:'Races and defining moments · Giro d’Italia', title:'When Froome attacked with 80 kilometres remaining', intro:'Chris Froome began stage 19 in fourth place. On the Colle delle Finestre he rode away alone and overturned a Giro that had appeared settled.', alt:'Chris Froome and Geraint Thomas during a stage race in 2018', caption:'Chris Froome with Geraint Thomas at the 2018 Tour de France. This contemporary portrait does not show the Finestre attack. Photograph: filip bossuyt, CC BY 2.0.', sections:[['Three minutes and 22 seconds','Before stage 19, Froome was fourth, 3 minutes 22 seconds behind Simon Yates. He had won on Monte Zoncolan, but had also lost time and crashed before the race. With two decisive days left, a controlled podium ride looked the more likely option.'],['The attack on gravel','Team Sky raised the pace on the Colle delle Finestre. When the road turned to gravel, Froome attacked with roughly 80 kilometres remaining. Tom Dumoulin, Thibaut Pinot and the other contenders had to choose between cooperation and saving their own strength. Yates quickly lost contact.'],['Three mountains alone','Finestre was not the finish. Froome still had Sestriere and the climb to Jafferau ahead. Behind him, the chase alternated between cooperation and hesitation. In front, he kept a pace that slowly turned a gamble into the race lead.'],['Pink in one day','Froome won the stage 3 minutes 23 seconds ahead of Dumoulin and took the pink jersey. He defended it the next day and became the Giro’s first British winner in Rome.'],['Data and old-fashioned risk','The move came from a team associated with watts, nutrition plans and controlled climbing. Yet the decade’s defining Giro move was a long solo attack with everything at risk. Technology could measure the effort. It could not ride the final 80 kilometres.']], sources:[['Giro d’Italia: Memories from Colle delle Finestre 2018','https://www.giroditalia.it/en/news/memories-from-colle-delle-finestre-2018-chris-froomes-legendary-attack/']] },
  'tignes-etapen-stoppet-2019': { year:'2019', label:'Races and defining moments · Tour de France', title:'When a Tour stage ended without a finish line', intro:'Egan Bernal attacked on the Col de l’Iseran. Farther ahead, hail and a landslide made the road to Tignes impassable, and the race had to be stopped.', alt:'Egan Bernal in the yellow jersey at the 2019 Tour de France', caption:'Egan Bernal in yellow after the shortened stage towards Tignes. Photograph: filip bossuyt, CC BY 2.0.', sections:[['The attack on the Iseran','On stage 19, Bernal attacked towards the Col de l’Iseran. Yellow-jersey holder Julian Alaphilippe was in difficulty while Bernal crossed the summit first and began the descent with a chance to take the race lead.'],['The road disappeared ahead','A violent storm struck farther along the route. Hail covered the tarmac, and mud and water made the road towards Tignes impassable. Riders first saw officials waving them to the side. The explanation came later.'],['Times from the summit','Race officials used the times at the Col de l’Iseran. No stage winner was declared, but the time gaps counted for the general classification. Bernal took yellow, while Alaphilippe’s long defence ended without the planned final climb.'],['A race without its usual ending','Stage racing depends on fixed points: a start, checkpoints and a finish line. At Tignes, safety mattered more than the format. The decision produced an unusual result, but the alternative was to send the peloton onto a road that was no longer passable.'],['Bernal’s Tour','The next Alpine stage was also shortened because of weather. Bernal kept the lead to Paris and, at 22, became the first Colombian Tour winner. His decisive attack ended not beneath a finish banner, but at a timing point on a mountain pass.']], sources:[['Tour de France: Stormy conditions shorten stage 19','https://www.letour.fr/en/news/2019/stormy-conditions-shorten-stage-19-bernal-in-the-lead/1282764']] },
  'gilbert-flandern-rundt-2017': { year:'2017', label:'Races and defining moments · Tour of Flanders', title:'When Philippe Gilbert rode away on the Oude Kwaremont', intro:'An attack more than 50 kilometres from the finish first looked like team tactics. Philippe Gilbert continued alone and reached Oudenaarde with his advantage intact.', alt:'A professional road-racing peloton in the 2010s', caption:'Professional racing in the 2010s was increasingly organised through radio and data. This photograph shows the 2012 Tour peloton, not the 2017 Tour of Flanders. Photograph: William Morice, CC BY 2.0.', sections:[['Quick-Step opens the race','Quick-Step Floors had several possible winners and used them actively. On the second passage of the Oude Kwaremont the team increased the pace. Gilbert escaped with a group before continuing alone with more than 50 kilometres remaining.'],['No waiting for the finale','A long solo ride in Flanders costs energy on cobbles, climbs and exposed roads. Gilbert could have waited for help or saved his attack for later. Instead, he maintained the pressure while the chase was divided by conflicting interests.'],['Sagan, Van Avermaet and the crash','Peter Sagan, Greg Van Avermaet and Oliver Naesen were among the strongest chasers. On the final Oude Kwaremont, Sagan crashed after making contact with a jacket hanging over the spectator barrier. Van Avermaet and Naesen were also brought down.'],['Alone in Oudenaarde','Gilbert reached the finish alone, ahead of Van Avermaet and Niki Terpstra. It was his first Tour of Flanders victory and another monument alongside Liège–Bastogne–Liège and the Tour of Lombardy.'],['Radio did not decide everything','Racing in the 2010s was often described as controlled. Gilbert showed that an early attack could still upset the plan. Team radio could provide time gaps. It could not make the chase agree.']], sources:[['Tour of Flanders: History and winners','https://www.rondevanvlaanderen.be/en/race/men-elite/history']] },
  'sram-etap-traadloese-gear': { year:'2015', label:'Bicycles and technical change · Gears', title:'When the gear cable disappeared', intro:'SRAM RED eTap moved the signal between the controls and derailleurs through the air. It changed operation, installation and the appearance of the racing bicycle.', alt:'A 2013 Scapin K6 carbon racing bicycle with mechanical Campagnolo gears', caption:'The collection’s 2013 Scapin K6 uses mechanical shifting and shows the immediate starting point before wireless road groups. Photograph: The Evolution of Bikes.', sections:[['Electronic was no longer new','Shimano had already made electronic shifting dependable in professional racing, and Campagnolo followed with EPS. Those systems used wires between controls, battery and derailleurs. SRAM chose another route.'],['A signal without a cable','RED eTap was announced in 2015 as a wireless 11-speed road group. Each control sent a coded signal, while front and rear derailleurs had their own batteries. SRAM called the connection Airea; it was neither ordinary Bluetooth nor ANT+.'],['Left easier, right harder','Operation was deliberately simple: the left control moved to an easier sprocket, the right to a harder one, and both together operated the front derailleur. Fewer commands made mistakes with cold fingers less likely.'],['A mechanic’s new work','Without gear cables, setup became easier on frames with complicated internal routing. Charging, pairing, battery status and software entered the toolbox instead. Mechanical adjustment did not disappear; it gained radio signals as company.'],['The clean frame','Wireless shifting suited the decade’s integrated carbon bicycles. Fewer cables gave designers more freedom and produced a cleaner appearance. Within a few years the system moved from novelty to a normal option on performance road bicycles.']], sources:[['SRAM: Company history — RED eTap announced in 2015','https://www.sram.com/fr/company/about/history'],['SRAM Support: the Airea wireless protocol','https://support.sram.com/hc/en-us/articles/6086267696795-Is-the-SRAM-eTap-wireless-protocol-considered-Bluetooth-or-ANT']] },
  'annie-londonderry-verden-rundt': { year:'1894–1895', label:'Cycling culture and everyday life · Women on bicycles', title:'Annie Londonderry turned a bicycle journey into a performance', intro:'Annie Cohen Kopchovsky left Boston on a heavy women’s bicycle and returned fifteen months later as Annie Londonderry. Advertising, clothing and good stories travelled with her.', alt:'Portrait of Annie Londonderry around 1896', caption:'Annie Cohen Kopchovsky, better known as Annie Londonderry, photographed in Boston around 1896. Studio Tourne · public domain.', sections:[
    ['A name could be sold','When 23-year-old Annie Cohen Kopchovsky left the Massachusetts State House on 25 June 1894, she also left a husband and three young children. Londonderry Lithia Spring Water paid her to carry its name, and Annie Londonderry was easier for newspapers to sell than Annie Kopchovsky. The journey was travel, advertising and a public performance from its first day.'],
    ['The first bicycle did not last','She began in a long skirt on a Columbia made for women. In Chicago she changed to a lighter Sterling with a top tube and adopted bloomers. The change was practical and public. At a time when women’s clothing and freedom of movement were being argued over, the bicycle gave a woman distance without requiring a coachman or husband.'],
    ['Around the world, using several forms of transport','Londonderry sailed from New York to Le Havre, crossed France to Marseille and continued by ship through the Mediterranean and Asia. She rode substantial sections, but did not pedal every mile around the globe. Trains and steamships were part of the route. The feat was also the ability to travel independently and keep public attention alive.'],
    ['The stories grew','She gave interviews, lectures and sold photographs while carrying advertisements. Tales of war, danger and pursuit changed from town to town. Not every detail fits, but exaggeration was part of the work. If audiences paid to hear about the journey, the journey had to be worth hearing about.'],
    ['Back in Boston','She returned on 24 September 1895 after roughly fifteen months. The trip was presented as a wager about whether a woman could circle the world and earn money on the way, although the wager itself is difficult to verify. What is certain is that Londonderry returned famous and soon wrote under the name The New Woman.'],
    ['The bicycle as an entry ticket','She is not remembered for a velodrome record. Her achievement was to use the bicycle as an entry into newspapers, theatres, sponsorship and travel. She sold her name, changed her clothes, took the train, rode on and supplied the press with a story. It was a very modern occupation.']
  ], sources:[['University of Toronto: Annie Londonderry’s Extraordinary Ride','https://wjudaism.library.utoronto.ca/index.php/wjudaism/article/download/3534/1591/5373'],['Wikimedia Commons: Annie Londonderry c. 1896','https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Annie_Londonderry_vers_1896.jpg']] },
  'tour-1947-rationering': { year:'1947', label:'Nutrition, training and science · Tour de France', title:'When the Tour returned to a hungry France', intro:'The Tour returned in 1947 after an eight-year absence. Its riders needed fuel for 4,640 kilometres while France cut the daily bread ration to 200 grams.', alt:'Arie Vooren finishing during the 1947 Tour de France', caption:'Arie Vooren finishes fourth on a stage of the 1947 Tour. Harry Sagers/Anefo · CC BY-SA 3.0 NL.', sections:[
    ['A race returns','On 25 June 1947 the Tour peloton gathered in Paris again. The previous edition had been held in 1939. Occupation, war and shortages had changed France in between. Jacques Goddet and L’Équipe put the Tour back on the road with 100 riders, 21 stages and 4,640 kilometres.'],
    ['Bread was still counted','Peace did not fill the shops. France’s wheat harvest failed in 1947, and the government reduced the daily bread ration from 300 to 250 and then 200 grams. Ration cards, queues and substitutes remained ordinary life. Spectators therefore watched an event of enormous energy consumption in a society where every slice of bread mattered.'],
    ['The race required its own supply line','A stage race cannot be ridden on a civilian ration. Teams and organisers secured food through hotels, local contacts and feeding arrangements. Menus varied but relied on bread, rice or pasta, meat, eggs, fruit, sugar and strong drinks. A working peloton gained access to calories that many people beside the road could not take for granted.'],
    ['A travelling organisation','National and regional teams moved from hotel to hotel with mechanics, soigneurs, journalists and the publicity caravan. It was a long way from today’s mobile kitchens, but large enough to create a supply system of its own. A missed feed or poor hotel meal could still be felt the following day.'],
    ['Robic won on the final day','Jean Robic began the last stage in third place, attacked with Edouard Fachleitner and gained enough time to win without previously wearing yellow. Here the wider setting matters: while France counted rations, the Tour displayed hunger, food and exhaustion as public theatre along the road.'],
    ['Normal life, almost','The Tour’s return suggested that the old calendar could begin again, but 1947 was not carefree. Rationing continued until 1949. The finish-line photographs show not simply a race returning, but a large travelling organisation crossing a country that still could not feed everyone freely.']
  ], sources:[['Tour de France history','https://www.letour.fr/en/history'],['U.S. State Department: France’s 1947 wheat and bread crisis','https://history.state.gov/historicaldocuments/frus1947v03/d268'],['Chemins de mémoire: rationing in France','https://www.cheminsdememoire.gouv.fr/en/node/10023']] },
  '7-eleven-europa': { year:'1985–1986', label:'Society and the times · Professional teams', title:'When 7-Eleven came to Europe', intro:'A US team named after convenience stores reached the Giro in 1985 and the Tour a year later. It had to learn Europe’s unwritten rules, but it had not come merely to watch.', alt:'Eddy Merckx team bicycle in 7-Eleven colours from 1989', caption:'A preserved Eddy Merckx team bicycle in 7-Eleven’s green, red and white colours from 1989. PD7400-pusher · public domain.', sections:[
    ['A team named after a shop','Jim Ochowicz founded the team in 1981 with Eric Heiden and George Mount. It began as an American amateur project with a conspicuous sponsor. After the 1984 Los Angeles Olympics, the ambition became professional and European. That meant entering a calendar where many rules were written between the lines.'],
    ['The Giro as the first test','The team entered the 1985 Giro with Mike Neel bringing European experience and Andy Hampsten on a short contract. Ron Kiefel won in Perugia and Hampsten later won at Gran Paradiso. Results did more than a polished presentation could: they showed the Americans could finish the work.'],
    ['A practical culture shock','European professional cycling meant small hotels, narrow roads, late meals, several languages and a fixed pecking order. The Americans arrived with Olympic backgrounds, jerseys resembling shop signs and less respect for old hierarchies. Tales of wrong orders and surprised mechanics grew around the team; not every detail is verifiable, but the collision of working cultures was real.'],
    ['The first Tour','In 1986, 7-Eleven became the first US-based Tour de France team. Canadian Alex Stieda collected enough bonus seconds to hold yellow, green, white and polka dots briefly. Davis Phinney won a bunch sprint the next day. In a weekend the team moved from curiosity to participant.'],
    ['Europe changed the team too','7-Eleven became more European in organisation and programme while retaining its American identity. Hampsten returned and won the Giro in 1988. European manufacturers such as Eddy Merckx supplied bicycles while the sponsor and much of the team’s character came from the United States.'],
    ['A new professional landscape','7-Eleven did not transform cycling alone, but made the change visible. Television, international sponsors and riders from more continents expanded the old western European environment. A convenience-store name on a Merckx frame first looked odd. Soon multinational teams looked normal.']
  ], sources:[['Tour de France: Alex Stieda’s day in yellow','https://www.letour.fr/en/news/2019/a-day-in-yellow-alex-stieda-vii-x/1276101'],['7-Eleven and the 1986 Tour','https://www.bikeraceinfo.com/riderhistories/7-eleven-1986.html'],['Motorola team guide 1995','https://www.bicycletourcompany.com/about_us/pdf/motorola95.pdf']] },
  'lotus-108-boardman-1992': { year:'1992', label:'The people behind cycling · Aerodynamics', title:'The bicycle without the usual triangle', intro:'Mike Burrows drew a carbon bicycle, cycling first said no, and Lotus prepared it for Chris Boardman’s pursuit of Olympic gold in Barcelona.', alt:'Black Lotus 108 track bicycle in the Design Museum', caption:'Lotus 108 with a carbon monocoque and single-sided fork. Ged Carroll · CC BY 2.0.', sections:[
    ['A shape without tubes','Mike Burrows worked with recumbents, aerodynamics and composites when he developed a track bicycle that did not resemble joined tubes. Its carbon monocoque was a structural shell shaped around airflow and loads. The familiar diamond frame and the usual two-sided fork were gone.'],
    ['The rules got in the way','The design was initially rejected because monocoque forms were not permitted. When the UCI changed the rules around 1990, the idea became useful again. Lotus Engineering had carbon-fibre experience from motor racing and could develop the construction to Olympic standards. Burrows supplied the concept; Lotus engineers made it a competition bicycle.'],
    ['Boardman was the other half','An aerodynamic bicycle is only fast if its rider can hold the position and deliver power. Chris Boardman was known for controlled preparation and attention to small gains. In the 4,000-metre individual pursuit, the rider must accelerate a large gear and then keep head, shoulders, arms and back working with the machine.'],
    ['Four minutes 24.496 seconds','In Barcelona Boardman reached the final against Jens Lehmann, caught the German before the distance was complete and secured Britain’s first Olympic cycling gold for 72 years. The time remains a number from the day; pictures of the black bicycle made the larger impression. It appeared to belong to another rulebook.'],
    ['Beyond the velodrome','The success produced the Lotus 110 road model and a brief period in which the brand represented the new carbon technology. Production was expensive and the bicycles never became common. They did not need to. The 108 showed that a racing bicycle could be designed as one aerodynamic form instead of a collection of tubes.'],
    ['The people inside the object','The story is often shortened to Boardman on a Lotus. The bicycle existed because Burrows retained an idea through regulatory resistance, Lotus industrialised it, and coaches, technicians and rider made it work. The fastest object resulted from many slow working hours. Mechanics often does.']
  ], sources:[['Design Museum: Lotus Type 108','https://designmuseum.org/exhibitions/cycle-revolution'],['Olympic Museum: Lotus bicycle and Mike Burrows','https://olympic-museum-artefacts.zetcom.net/en/collection/item/102185/'],['Lane Motor Museum: Lotus and the Burrows design','https://www.lanemotormuseum.org/collection/bicycles/item/lotus-type-110tt/']] },
  'tourmad-i-1930erne': { year:'1930s', label:'Nutrition, training and science · Tour de France', title:'When the Tour had to provide dinner', intro:'When the Tour de France introduced national teams in 1930, the organiser also assumed responsibility for the riders’ food and lodging. Feeding became more organised, though hardly simple.', alt:'Learco Guerra during the 1930 Tour de France', caption:'Learco Guerra during the 1930 Tour, the first edition with national teams and supplies organised by the race. Bibliothèque nationale de France · public domain.', sections:[
    ['Dinner became an organiser’s problem','Before 1930, trade teams supplied riders, bicycles, helpers and money. They also arranged food and hotels. Henri Desgrange replaced them with national teams. Riders received identical yellow bicycles, while the Tour organisation paid for lodging, transport and meals. The publicity caravan, introduced that same year, helped meet the bill. The new sporting system therefore began in the kitchen as well as on the road.'],
    ['Breakfast was meant to feel substantial','A long stage could occupy most of the day. Riders ate accordingly: meat, eggs, bread, rice, soup and coffee reflected ordinary ideas about strength. Starting with steak or another hot dish was not unusual. Carbohydrate was certainly present, but nobody prescribed an exact number of grams per hour. Riders ate to be full and to have something to work on. Sometimes they began the stage with rather too much of it.'],
    ['The musette balanced the day','Food bags supplied bread, fruit, chocolate, sugar, rice or pieces of meat. Their contents depended on the team, hotel and local supply. Some riders delayed eating for fear of a heavy stomach; others ate whenever they could. The modern instruction to consume a measured dose of carbohydrate every twenty minutes still lay decades ahead.'],
    ['Lapébie did not follow the steak rule','The 1937 winner Roger Lapébie was later described as almost vegetarian. That is a useful warning against inventing one menu for the entire peloton. Riders swore by meat, rice, milk, eggs or fruit in different combinations. A winner’s habit could quickly become common practice even when the reason for victory lay elsewhere. Experience and superstition were close neighbours.'],
    ['The beer stop is a good story','The best-known episode came on stage 17 in 1935. The story says that much of the peloton stopped for beer while Julien Moineau rode on and won by a large margin. Details vary, but alcohol at races was not extraordinary. Beer and wine offered liquid, calories and a brief respite. That does not mean the field always raced drunk. It means the boundary between feeding and refreshment looked different.'],
    ['Vitamins entered by the side door','By the end of the decade, riders and teams spoke more systematically about vitamins and diet. Vitamin use was discussed at the 1939 Tour as a possible aid to performance. The new interest did not immediately replace old habits. A rider might receive a vitamin tablet, a large steak and a glass of wine on the same day without seeing a contradiction. Science was arriving with mixed luggage.'],
    ['From hotel kitchen to sports laboratory','The decade’s main change was not one dish. Feeding became an explicit part of race organisation. If the Tour selected the teams, it also had to provide beds and calories. Soigneurs, hotel cooks and helpers became essential. Nutrition remained imprecise, but it was no longer only the rider’s private problem. Modern chefs and measured plans were far away, yet the direction had been established.']
  ], sources:[['KOERS Museum: feeding during the Tour','https://servicekoers.be/verhalen/bevoorrading-tijdens-de-tour'],['L’Équipe: a history of Tour meals','https://www.lequipe.fr/Cyclisme-sur-route/Article/La-copieuse-histoire-des-repas-du-tour-de-france/1226539'],['1930 Tour: national teams, food and lodging','https://www.bikeraceinfo.com/tdf/tdf1930.html']] },
  'faliero-masi-vigorelli': { year:'1949–1969', label:'The people behind cycling · Frame building', title:'The tailor beneath the Vigorelli', intro:'Faliero Masi worked in a small shop beneath the stands of Milan’s Vigorelli velodrome. Riders were measured, steel was cut and famous bicycles left carrying other names.', alt:'Jacques Anquetil during the 1966 Tour de France', caption:'Jacques Anquetil in 1966. Ten years earlier he set the hour record at the Vigorelli on a frame built by Faliero Masi. Eric Koch/Anefo · CC BY-SA 3.0 NL.', sections:[
    ['The shop lay beneath the banking','The Vigorelli was more than a steep wooden track. Beneath its stands were changing rooms, storage and, from 1949, Faliero Masi’s workshop. When riders trained overhead, Masi could hear the track and see the people his bicycles had to fit. He had raced and worked as a mechanic, experience that taught him a frame needed more than correct numbers on paper. It had to work beneath a particular body.'],
    ['They called him the tailor','Masi became known as il sarto, the tailor. The comparison concerned fit. He measured legs, torso, shoulders and position and adjusted tube angles and lengths so a rider could work naturally. Steel rule, angle finder, observation and experience came before laser fitting. Two riders of equal height did not necessarily need the same bicycle. One sat low and still; another moved constantly.'],
    ['Anquetil’s bicycle did not suit the track','Before Jacques Anquetil’s 1956 hour attempt, his original bicycle proved unsuitable for the Vigorelli’s tight bends, according to the velodrome’s history. Masi rapidly built another. On 29 June Anquetil covered 46.159 kilometres and beat Fausto Coppi’s record. However colourful later retellings became, the essential point is firm: Masi built the record bicycle, and local frame-building knowledge directly affected a world record.'],
    ['The down-tube name was not always Masi','Professional teams contracted bicycle sponsors. A rider could therefore use a custom Masi frame carrying the sponsor’s transfer. The practice was neither unique to Masi nor necessarily a great secret within the peloton. It shows the difference between the brand the public saw and the craftsman who selected the geometry and joined the tubes. Mechanics knew the difference; they maintained the bicycle afterwards.'],
    ['The 1960s passed through the door','Masi built for Federico Bahamontes around his 1959 Tour victory and for Gastone Nencini in 1960. Rik Van Looy, Antonio Maspes and later Eddy Merckx followed. This does not make every one of their bicycles or victories a Masi product; teams, manufacturers and seasons changed. It does make the shop a junction where professionals could correct a position or order a frame for a particular task.'],
    ['A steel frame was a collaboration','Good tubes were only the beginning. Mitres had to meet, lugs had to fit, brazing had to heat the joint without damaging the steel and the rear triangle had to be aligned. Then came paint, components and the rider’s first test. If the bicycle felt wrong, the drawing was not finished simply because the paint was dry. Working close to riders and mechanics allowed experience to return quickly to the bench.'],
    ['The man behind another name','Today, Masi is plainly written on collectors’ bicycles. In the 1960s peloton his work could remain beneath a sponsor’s logo. That suited the mechanic’s role: the performance needed to be visible; its maker did not. Beneath the Vigorelli, Faliero Masi could switch on the workshop light and alter a dimension by a few millimetres. Above him, those millimetres might become a world record or simply a better working day.']
  ], sources:[['Vigorelli: history and the Masi workshop','https://www.vigorelli.eu/il-vigorelli/storia'],['Vigorelli: Anquetil’s record bicycle','https://www.vigorelli.eu/vecchio_sito/storia/stcamp.html'],['Masi historical timeline','https://masibikes.com/pages/history']] },
  'da-fjernsynet-fandt-touren': { year:'1948–1959', label:'Society and the times · Media', title:'When television found the Tour', intro:'Radio had long given the Tour a voice. Television first had to learn how to follow a race that moved every day and regularly disappeared among the mountains.', alt:'Riders and spectators at a feed zone during the 1958 Tour de France', caption:'The 1958 Tour was the first edition to show mountain racing live on television. Here riders pass a feed zone. Nationaal Archief/Anefo · CC0.', sections:[
    ['A live broadcast from a fixed finish line','In 1948 a Tour finish was broadcast live on French television for the first time. It sounds modest now, but the assignment suited contemporary equipment badly. Cameras, control vehicle, cables and signal links had to remain in one place while the race approached on an uncertain timetable. Viewers received the finish, not the whole stage. Radio, newspapers and later film still told most of the day.'],
    ['The Tour was a television problem','A stadium could be wired and tested in advance. The Tour began somewhere new every morning. Mountains blocked signals, roads were narrow, and power and connections had to be assembled temporarily. A motorcycle could film close to the riders, but its picture still had to reach a receiver and then the audience. Television crews did not merely follow the race. They built a new production site each day.'],
    ['The mountains reached the screen in 1958','In 1958 mountain climbs were shown live for the first time. Viewers could now watch faces, rhythm and gaps as they developed instead of waiting for evening film or the next newspaper. Charly Gaul’s Tour victory therefore belonged to a different public setting than earlier climbers’ triumphs. Pictures remained monochrome, selective and unstable, but the climb had become shared live time.'],
    ['The helicopter supplied a new view','The television production introduced helicopters in 1959. At first they were as important for relaying signals as for the aerial pictures now associated with the Tour. From above, a camera could place the field in the landscape and follow a longer section of road. Signals could also cross terrain that defeated ground transmission. It was costly and awkward. It worked often enough to continue.'],
    ['The race began thinking in pictures','Television did not immediately redraw the route, but it gave certain places new value. A hairpin road, a dense crowd or a finish with room for equipment could provide memorable pictures. Organisers increasingly wanted drama that could be seen. Riders learned that an attack was no longer reported only later; it might unfold in living rooms while their legs still hurt.'],
    ['Commentators explained what remained invisible','Cameras showed only part of the field. Punctures, escapes and tactical moves happened outside the frame. Commentators assembled information from race radio, motorcycles and the finish while explaining flickering images. Television did not remove the storyteller. It gave that person more fragments to hold together. The method persists, even as cameras and data sources multiply.'],
    ['From novelty to daily programme','By the end of the 1950s, the entire Tour was not broadcast live from start to finish. The basic form nevertheless existed: mobile cameras, relays, helicopter, commentators and a production travelling with the race. The Tour remained a bicycle race and a newspaper story. It had also become a recurring television event, changing who could follow it and what a July moment increasingly needed to do: be visible.']
  ], sources:[['UCI TV Production Guide: first live Tour finish in 1948','https://assets.ctfassets.net/761l7gh5x5an/6CZrPOFvHrTlhFnDN1tnA5/fa6268c7bf9145076698fd5a6b3efad4/guidedeproductiontv-tvproductionguide_neutral.pdf'],['INA: television crew at the 1959 Tour','https://catalogue.ina.fr/doc/TV-RADIO/DA_CAF97501623/tour-de-france-cycliste-l-equipe-de-television-sur-le-tour']] },
  'campagnolo-cambio-corsa': { year:'1946', label:'Bicycles and technical change · Gear shifting', title:'The gear change with a loose rear wheel', intro:'Cambio Corsa offered several gears without a roadside stop. In return, the rider operated two levers, back-pedalled and released the rear wheel while the bicycle was still moving.', alt:'The two Campagnolo Cambio Corsa control levers on the collection’s Stella Veneta', caption:'The two levers on the collection’s Stella Veneta. The long lever releases and locks the rear wheel; the short one guides the chain between sprockets. Photograph: The Evolution of Bikes.', sections:[
    ['The right hand reached behind the saddle','A modern rider shifts with a small movement at the brake lever. Cambio Corsa placed its controls on the right seat stay. Two metal rods rose towards the rider’s hand. One operated the rear axle quick release; the other moved a fork-shaped chain guide across the sprockets. There was no spring-loaded derailleur to absorb changes in chain length. The rear wheel therefore had to move as well.'],
    ['Why the wheel had to be released','Moving the chain from a small sprocket to a larger one demands more chain. Cambio Corsa dealt with this by allowing the hub to slide in long horizontal dropouts. Toothed axle washers travelled in the slots after the quick release was opened. Chain and pedalling forces helped the wheel find its new position. Only then could the rider secure it again.'],
    ['A particular sequence','The change required calm practice. The rider eased the load, reached back and opened the axle with the long lever. The short lever pushed the chain towards another sprocket while the rider back-pedalled. The wheel moved to establish usable chain tension, and the axle was locked. The wrong sequence or a change under full power could make the chain object. An improperly secured wheel was a much larger problem.'],
    ['Progress without stopping','The arrangement looks awkward, but it solved a real problem. A flip-flop rear wheel required the rider to stop, loosen both nuts, remove the wheel and reverse it. Cambio Corsa made several ratios available while moving. On a long climb or rolling course that mattered. Shifting had become part of racing rather than a roadside repair, though not yet an effortless action.'],
    ['A machine between eras','Campagnolo had developed quick releases and moving-hub gears in the 1930s. The form known as Cambio Corsa arrived after the war and is generally dated to 1946. It belongs to a brief period when racing bicycles had several rear sprockets but not yet the derailleur that would keep wheel position independent of gear choice.'],
    ['Skill was part of the mechanism','The rider removed one hand from the bar, found two levers behind the body and altered the rear wheel’s clamping while riding narrow tyres on rough roads. Experts made it look fluid. Beginners found little forgiveness. Cambio Corsa therefore records the mechanical skills expected of riders as clearly as it records Campagnolo’s ingenuity.'],
    ['The Stella Veneta makes it visible','On the collection’s Stella Veneta, rods, chain guide and long dropouts remain in full view. Nothing is hidden inside a lever or electronic cable. Moving one part shows why the next must follow. The bicycle turns an unfamiliar transitional mechanism into something tangible.'],
    ['The parallelogram closed the chapter','Around 1950, Campagnolo Gran Sport made the spring-loaded parallelogram derailleur the new model. It moved and tensioned the chain while the wheel stayed fixed. Cambio Corsa quickly looked old, but it was not unimportant. It was a daring bridge between reversing a wheel by the roadside and the modern shift.']
  ], sources:[['Museo Nicolis: 1947 Bianchi with Campagnolo gear','https://www.museonicolis.com/en/bianchi-1947-ufficiale/'],['Campagnolo company history','https://www.campagnolo.com/us-en/company/history/'],['The Evolution of Bikes: Campagnolo Cambio Corsa','https://teob.dk/en/components/campagnolo-cambio-corsa/']] },
  'shimano-dura-ace-ax': { year:'1980–1983', label:'Bicycles and technical change · Aerodynamics', title:'When even the pedal thread had to be aerodynamic', intro:'Shimano built a wind tunnel and gave Dura-Ace AX smooth forms. The experiment reached the pedal itself and showed how far a complete technical idea could be pushed.', alt:'Shimano 600 AX Dyna-Drive crank and pedal on the collection’s Koga-Miyata Gentsracer-Aero', caption:'The collection’s Koga-Miyata carries Shimano 600 AX with Dyna-Drive. This is the related 600 group, not Dura-Ace AX, but it shows how the AX ideas spread beyond the flagship. Photograph: The Evolution of Bikes.', sections:[
    ['Aerodynamics became a system','Around 1980, a fast bicycle was no longer defined only by a light frame and good bearings. Shimano tried to treat the component group as one airflow. Dura-Ace AX received rounded, smooth shapes with edges, bolts and protrusions reduced or covered. Brakes, hubs, controls, crank and pedals belonged to the same argument.'],
    ['From Dura-Ace to AX','Shimano introduced Dura-Ace in 1973 and EX in 1978. The 7300 AX series followed in 1980. The Japanese company wanted to challenge Europe’s leading groups with more than price. It built a wind tunnel and made aerodynamics the group’s clear identity. AX was both a technical experiment and a claim to leadership.'],
    ['Small gains across the bicycle','Sharp transitions were rounded and several fittings gained covers or lower profiles. Brake and control shapes considered airflow, while hubs and shifting parts followed the same visual language. Each gain could be small. The point was the sum—and the possibility of selling the bicycle as a coordinated aerodynamic system.'],
    ['Dyna-Drive lowered the foot','Dyna-Drive was the most conspicuous idea. Its bearings were not arranged around a conventional spindle outside the crank. An oversized thread allowed the platform to sit closer to the centre of the axle. Shimano claimed a lower foot and reduced frontal area. The design required dedicated cranks and pedals, so it could not simply be mixed with standard equipment.'],
    ['Flagship and sibling','Dura-Ace AX led the project, but its ideas travelled into 600 AX. The collection’s 1983 Koga-Miyata Gentsracer-Aero carries Shimano 600 AX and Dyna-Drive. It does not document Dura-Ace itself. It shows something equally useful: the aero project became a product family fitted to high-quality production bicycles.'],
    ['When a system limits choice','Special solutions may be elegant while making replacements difficult. Dyna-Drive did not use a normal pedal thread, and several AX parts occupied their own technical world. Mechanics had less freedom to combine components from different years and makers. The short lifespan later made AX fascinating to collectors—and demonstrated that an ingenious solution does not automatically become a standard.'],
    ['Shifting became the stronger story','Dura-Ace 7400 arrived in 1984. Accurate shifting, followed by Shimano’s indexed SIS system, became more important than the special AX appearance. Many smooth AX forms disappeared, but Shimano did not stop thinking in systems. Compatibility among controls, derailleur, chain and sprockets became one of its greatest strengths.'],
    ['A short experiment with a long idea','Dura-Ace AX did not solve aerodynamic bicycles once and for all. Modern tunnels measure differently, and today’s components face other constraints. Yet the group made every small part available for questioning. AX’s precise solutions were short-lived. Its method of thinking was not.']
  ], sources:[['Shimano: Dura-Ace AX 7300','https://www.shimano.com/en/100th/history/products/14.html'],['Shimano: 600 AX','https://www.shimano.com/en/100th/history/products/17.html'],['Shimano AX service instructions','https://si.shimano.com/en/pdfs/si/L-79/SI-L-79-AX-000-00-ENG.pdf'],['The Evolution of Bikes: Koga-Miyata Gentsracer-Aero','https://teob.dk/en/bikes/koga-miyata-gentsracer-aero/']] },
  'liege-bastogne-liege-1980': { year:'1980', label:'Races and defining moments · Liège–Bastogne–Liège', title:'The day snow emptied Liège–Bastogne–Liège', intro:'A field of 174 began in the cold. Only 21 finished, while Bernard Hinault rode roughly 80 kilometres alone and suffered lasting damage to his hands.', alt:'Bernard Hinault photographed during the 1982 Tour de France', caption:'Bernard Hinault at the 1982 Tour de France. This contemporary portrait does not show the snow race in Liège. Marcel Antonisse/Anefo, Nationaal Archief · CC0.', sections:[
    ['Zero degrees at the start','On 20 April 1980, 174 riders started Liège–Bastogne–Liège in temperatures around freezing. Soon the rain became snow. Riders, road and convoy gradually disappeared into a white-grey landscape where the immediate problem was no longer position in the peloton but whether hands could still operate brakes.'],
    ['The field disappeared','Before the race had properly reached Bastogne, roughly two-thirds of the starters were gone. Cold passed through wool, gloves and rain capes. Glasses misted, shoes filled with water and fingers struggled to open food. Some sought team cars and cafés. Others continued because a group remained visible ahead.'],
    ['Hinault also considered stopping','Bernard Hinault was the reigning Tour winner, but the weather did not distinguish stars from domestiques. He later recalled planning to abandon at the feed zone. Instead he continued. The choice became part of the legend, though he did not yet know he would win. At that point he was simply still riding.'],
    ['The attack began far from home','Rudy Pevenage and Ludo Peeters had been ahead when Hinault raised the pace with just under 100 kilometres remaining. On the Côte de la Haute-Levée he left his final companions. Roughly 80 solo kilometres remained through snow and meltwater. Keeping the body working also became a way of preserving warmth.'],
    ['Nine minutes and 24 seconds','Hinault reached Liège 9 minutes 24 seconds before Hennie Kuiper, with Ronny Claes third. Only 21 of 174 starters were classified as finishers. The statistic is memorable, but says little about how those last riders looked or how small a result sheet feels beside people trying merely to reach warmth.'],
    ['The hands paid','Hinault’s fingers were so badly affected that a warm bath did not end the story. The UCI records lasting loss of sensation in one finger on each hand. The victory was therefore not only proof of resilience. It also showed that the body can carry the cost after the crowd leaves.'],
    ['Helpers had few answers','Soigneurs offered warm drinks, spare clothing and towels, but assistance on the road was limited. Clothing could be soaked before the next hand-up, and a frozen rider might struggle to put on a jacket. Modern racing has better garments and formal extreme-weather procedures. In 1980, the decision was more loosely shared among organisers, teams and riders.'],
    ['More than a heroic tale','The race is often called Hinault’s greatest Classic victory, and his attack deserves its place. Yet the story becomes poorer if it is only about one man being tougher than the weather. There were 153 abandonments and the winner suffered lasting injury. The day shows cycling’s stubbornness and its old habit of first asking whether something could be completed, then asking what it cost.']
  ], sources:[['UCI: 1980 Liège–Bastogne–Liège','https://www.uci.org/article/1980-liege-bastogne-liege-hinault-s-greatest-classic-victory/7xtOJL3SHb5il6TpV5oaZW'],['ProCyclingStats: 1980 result','https://www.procyclingstats.com/race/liege-bastogne-liege/1980/result']] },
  'major-taylor-verdensmester': { year:'1899', label:'Riders of the period · Track cycling', title:'Major Taylor also had to win the right to race', intro:'Marshall “Major” Taylor became world sprint champion in 1899. His route there passed through records, packed velodromes and a cycling establishment that repeatedly tried to exclude him.', alt:'Marshall Major Taylor on his track bicycle around 1906 or 1907', caption:'Marshall “Major” Taylor photographed around 1906–1907, several years after his world title. Reproduced in his 1928 autobiography; public domain.', sections:[
    ['A world champion at Queen’s Park','At the 1899 World Championships in Montreal, Marshall Walter Taylor won the one-mile sprint. He was twenty. The result made him the first Black American cycling world champion and one of the earliest Black world champions in modern sport. Behind that line in the record book lay a career in which he repeatedly proved both his speed and his right to enter.'],
    ['The name came from a bicycle shop','Taylor grew up in Indianapolis and worked at a bicycle shop as a boy. He performed riding tricks in a military-style uniform and became known as “Major”. The display supplied a name and demonstrated control, but speed on the track opened professional racing.'],
    ['To Worcester with Birdie Munger','Bicycle manufacturer and former racer Louis “Birdie” Munger recognised Taylor’s ability and became an important ally. Taylor moved to Worcester, Massachusetts, with its strong bicycle industry and better racing opportunities. He turned professional in 1896. Records and victories followed in a sport attracting large crowds to indoor and outdoor tracks.'],
    ['Track racing was major entertainment','A sprint match in the 1890s could be a headline event with prize money and dense grandstands. Riders used tactics, slow openings and sudden acceleration before throwing bicycles at the line. Taylor was compact, explosive and hard to pass. His fame grew while the bicycle became a symbol of individual freedom. That freedom was not offered equally.'],
    ['The colour line followed him','The League of American Wheelmen excluded Black members in 1894. Its racing arm operated under different rules, but discrimination remained. Taylor was refused by tracks and hotels, struggled to obtain meals while travelling and faced organisers and competitors who did not want a Black winner. Racism was not a footnote; it shaped where he could race and how each victory was received.'],
    ['Opposition became physical','Ordinary hard sprinting could merge with deliberate efforts to box him in or force him from the track. Taylor received threats and was assaulted by a rival after at least one race. He continued, while spending energy on more than training and tactics. His victories were over named riders in real races while the system around them made his work harder.'],
    ['Faith created another boundary','Taylor was a devout Baptist and refused Sunday racing. Many major meetings fell on Sundays, costing starts and income and complicating tours abroad. He became American sprint champion in 1900 and won important races in Europe and Australia, but opportunity never arrived as one unbroken line. Some events he declined; others were denied.'],
    ['Fame lasted less than the achievement','Taylor lost much of his wealth after retirement and died in Chicago in 1932, aged 53. He was initially buried in an unmarked grave. Later riders and cycling organisations restored his story to public memory. His speed deserves remembrance. So does the work required merely to reach the starting line. Major Taylor did not only win races; he forced a closed sport to record that he had won them.']
  ], sources:[['UCI: track world championship palmarès','https://assets.ctfassets.net/761l7gh5x5an/6LNcQK2kxLKAWoViX1ZWe6/9b4b56bd4b5b41727ed329aece4bbcd5/elite-track-world-championships---palmar-s.pdf'],['Major Taylor Association: biography','https://www.majortaylorassociation.org/who.shtml'],['National Endowment for the Humanities: Major Taylor','https://www.neh.gov/article/meet-major-taylor-fastest-man-two-wheels'],['Major Taylor Association: League of American Wheelmen colour line','https://majortaylorassociation.org/LAB_text.htm'],['Wikimedia Commons: portrait from 1906–1907','https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Major_Taylor,_1906-1907.jpg']] },
  'ugo-bianchi-let-banescykel-1948': { year:'1948', label:'Mechanics with consequences · Track cycling', title:'The track bicycle said to weigh 3.48 kilograms', intro:'The scale is said to have stopped at 3.48 kilograms. That is an extraordinary figure for a metal track bicycle built in 1948—and precisely why the story needs checking before we ride away with it.', alt:'Fausto Coppi during his 1942 hour record at the Vigorelli', caption:'Fausto Coppi during his 1942 Vigorelli hour record. His Legnano track bicycle was built by Ugo Bianchi and weighed about 7.5 kilograms. This is not the alleged 3.48-kilogram machine from 1948. Photograph: unknown/Wikimedia Commons, public domain.', sections:[
    ['A number worth weighing twice','A track bicycle stands on the workshop floor. No brakes, derailleur, mudguards or bottle cages. The story goes that Italian mechanic Ugo Bianchi built such a machine in 1948 and that it weighed only 3.480 kilograms. The claim was later repeated with Coppi mechanic Pinella De Grandi as part of its line of transmission. I have not found a contemporary weighing certificate. The figure should remain a fine workshop story rather than an approved record.'],
    ['Ugo Bianchi was real enough','The maker is easier to place. Ugo Bianchi worked with Legnano’s racing bicycles and De Grandi regarded him as a teacher. He also built the Legnano track bicycle used by Fausto Coppi for his 1942 hour record at the Vigorelli. That surviving bicycle gives us firm ground: a mechanic who understood the track and worked for riders able to feel a few millimetres or a reluctant bearing.'],
    ['The track removed a few problems','A track bicycle is a useful starting point when everything unnecessary must go. There is a fixed hub, one sprocket and one chainring. No brakes, cables or shifting mechanism. The wheels run on an even surface rather than potholes and kerbs. Parts disappear, but loads do not. A standing acceleration twists handlebar, bottom bracket and rear wheel. A component filed too thin may offer no warning.'],
    ['Gram by gram','We do not know the alleged bicycle’s construction, so precise explanations would be guesswork. The period toolbox is familiar: thin tubes, light wooden rims, aluminium parts, shortened bolts and fittings worked by hand. Every piece could lose weight. Eventually the mechanic faced the better question: was enough material left for the rider who had to use it?'],
    ['Coppi’s bicycle weighed more than twice as much','Coppi’s documented 1942 record bicycle weighed about 7.5 kilograms. It had wooden rims, aluminium pedals and simple track equipment, yet had to survive an hour at speed. Six years later, Bianchi’s ultra-light machine supposedly weighed less than half as much. Something very light can certainly be built. We do not know whether 3.48 kilograms meant a complete, rideable bicycle with tyres and pedals—or whether it entered a proper race.'],
    ['Record machine or workshop exercise','Without an owner, photographs and a contemporary weighing, we cannot say whether it was a racing tool, experiment or display piece. That matters. A machine can demonstrate how far material may be pushed without being suitable for a powerful sprinter. To my mind, this makes the story better. Forget shouting “record”; watch Ugo Bianchi with the file, deciding where the next gram might come from.'],
    ['The number keeps its asterisk','For now, 3.48 kilograms is a handed-down figure. Ugo Bianchi’s work for Legnano and Coppi’s record bicycle are better supported; the 1948 machine is not. Still, the story shows weight hunting well before carbon fibre and electronic scales. Parts were measured, filed and tested. Somewhere in the workshop, someone may have lifted the bicycle with one hand and smiled. Until a weighing slip appears, that is enough.']
  ], sources:[['Biciclette d’Epoca: Coppi Bike Story and Ugo Bianchi','https://biciclettedepoca.net/news/biciclette/coppi-bike-story-i-parte/'],['Treccani: Fausto Coppi and the 7.5-kilogram record bicycle','https://www.treccani.it/enciclopedia/fausto-coppi_(Dizionario-Biografico)/'],['Museo del Ghisallo: collection and Coppi record bicycle','https://www.museodelghisallo.it/en/museum/'],['Wikimedia Commons: Coppi at the Vigorelli in 1942','https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fausto_Coppi_-_record_dell%27ora_--_hour_record_-_1942_(1_of_2).jpg']] },
  'keetie-van-oosten-hage': { year:'1968–1979', label:'Riders of the period · Women’s cycling', title:'Keetie won almost everything that existed', intro:'Keetie van Oosten-Hage became world champion on road and track, set an hour record and kept teaching. The question was not whether she could win, but where she was allowed to race.', alt:'Keetie van Oosten-Hage at the Dutch championships in 1979', caption:'Keetie van Oosten-Hage at the Dutch championships in Geulle on 23 June 1979. Koen Suyk/Anefo, Nationaal Archief · CC0.', sections:[
    ['Four sisters and bicycles on the car','Cornelia Hage grew up in Zeeland in a family with several racing sisters. Bella began first and Keetie followed. The family drove from event to event with bicycles on the car. Dutch women’s races were often short, while Belgian races could be longer and harder. It was a practical education: find a race, get there, race hard and perform much of the surrounding work yourself.'],
    ['Imola in 1968','At the 1968 World Championships in Imola she still raced as Keetie Hage. She was nineteen. Two days before the road race she struck a van while training and injured a finger. An Italian soigneur helped her return and have it treated. She started and won the sprint from the small surviving group. It was her first road world championship.'],
    ['A career on two surfaces','Hage was not only a road rider. From 1966 through 1977 she won the Dutch individual pursuit title twelve consecutive times. She became world pursuit champion four times between 1975 and 1979. On the road she took another world title in 1976 and numerous national championships. The combination demanded different gears, positions and rhythms. It suited her rather well.'],
    ['43.082 kilometres','On 16 September 1978 she rode the hour record in Munich. The final distance was 43.082 kilometres, with new five, ten and twenty-kilometre records along the way. The hour figure survived for more than two decades before Anna Wilson improved it in 2000. It shows her level and also that women’s cycling produced elite performances long before it provided an elite programme.'],
    ['No Olympics and no Tour','Women received a road world championship in 1958, but women’s cycling did not enter the Olympic programme until 1984. Nor was there a stable women’s Tour de France during her career. International stage races were scarce and prize money small. Van Oosten-Hage later recalled that a criterium victory might pay roughly enough for a new tubular tyre. She kept working as a teacher. The world champion had an ordinary timetable beside racing.'],
    ['Training was largely her own problem','Daily support was limited. A world championship might supply massage and team management; regular racing did not automatically bring soigneurs, doctors and precise plans. She later described the method as riding hard and listening to her body. Structured schedules reached her only late in her career. This was not romantic freedom. It was a sport that had not decided to spend many resources on its best women.'],
    ['The bicycle went into the shed','She ended her career on 20 October 1979, the same year she again won the world pursuit title. She did not stop because she could no longer compete. After more than 200 victories, the largest opportunities later generations received still did not exist. Her story is easily reduced to titles. The work around them is more revealing: teaching, the family car, limited support and a calendar too small for the rider. She won almost everything available. There should have been more.']
  ], sources:[['UCI: Keetie van Oosten-Hage and the 1968 Worlds','https://www.uci.org/article/keetie-van-oosten-hage-s-imola-the-1968-uci-road-world-championships-sparked-a-superb-career/1Y8VRpV3Mz6G6lD9BfKNU1'],['KOERS Museum: portrait and interview','https://servicekoers.be/verhalen/portret-van-keetie-van-oosten-hage'],['Encyclopaedia of Zeeland: hour record and retirement','https://encyclopedievanzeeland.nl/Keetie_van_Oosten-Hage']] },
  'da-pulsen-kom-med-ud-paa-landevejen': { year:'1982–1989', label:'Food, training and science · Heart-rate monitoring', title:'When heart rate joined the rider on the road', intro:'A chest strap and a wrist receiver let riders follow the heart without stopping to count beats with their fingers.', alt:'Illustration of an early-1980s road cyclist wearing a chest strap and heart-rate receiver', caption:'Period-style illustration of an early wireless heart-rate monitor. Created for this article; not historical documentation.', sections:[
    ['A number in the middle of the road','Beneath the jersey, a narrow strap touches the chest; on the wrist, the receiver is larger than an ordinary watch. Its number does not show speed but the effort required to hold it. In the early 1980s, that was unusual information to carry on a training ride.'],
    ['From ski track to wrist','Polar was founded in Finland in 1977 following work on measuring heart rate wirelessly during exercise. The Sport Tester PE2000 reached the market in 1982. Electrodes in the chest strap detected the heartbeat and transmitted it to a receiver. Measurement could now follow the athlete through an entire effort rather than happen only at a stop or in a laboratory.'],
    ['The first circle was small','Polar describes its 1980s users as a limited group of elite athletes. One concrete early case was Finnish orienteer Kari Sallinen, who used the monitor with coach Jukka Kalliokoski in summer 1983. Cyclists gained the same advantage: a ride could be divided into easier and harder periods even when wind and hills made speed a poor guide.'],
    ['Distance was no longer the whole answer','A training diary might say 180 kilometres. Heart rate added another question: how were they ridden? Coaches could work more systematically with intensity, intervals and recovery. Yet the number also responded to heat, dehydration, nerves and fatigue. The monitor replaced neither the coach’s eye nor the rider’s experience.'],
    ['Before the power meter','Power meters later showed work directly in watts; heart rate showed the body’s response. In the 1980s, watts did not sit on every handlebar. The early Polar made part of the laboratory portable. It was not an answer sheet, but the beginning of a more measured training day.']
  ], sources:[['Polar: How heart rate monitors changed endurance sports','https://www.polar.com/us-en/guide/how-heart-rate-monitors-changed-endurance-sports'],['Polar: 40 years of firsts','https://www.polar.com/blog/40-years-of-incredible-firsts-polar-history/'],['Google Patents: Seppo Säynäjäkangas’ pulse transmitter','https://patents.google.com/patent/USD278746S/en'],['Polar Sport Tester user manual','https://support.polar.com/e_manuals/sport-tester/polar-sport-tester-user-manual-english.pdf']] },
  'peppino-drali-bianchi-ved-filebaenken': { year:'1946–1970s', label:'People behind cycling · Framebuilding', title:'Bianchi on the down tube, Drali at the file bench', intro:'The down tube said Bianchi. Giuseppe “Peppino” Drali stood at the file bench in Milan. A brand name rarely tells the full story of the hands behind a racing frame.', alt:'Illustration of an Italian framebuilder filing a celeste racing frame', caption:'Period-style illustration of a Milan frame workshop. It does not depict Peppino Drali and is not historical documentation.', sections:[
    ['The name and the hand','A frame rests in the jig. The joints have been brazed, but excess material still needs filing and the tubes must be checked. When the finished bicycle carried Bianchi on the down tube, a customer might assume it had all happened inside the large factory. Some special frames took another route.'],
    ['Carlo and young Peppino','Drali’s company history traces the relationship to Peppino’s father, Carlo, who worked closely with Bianchi’s racing department. It says Carlo and young Peppino helped prepare a Bianchi for Fausto Coppi before Milan–Sanremo in 1946. Coppi won. The exact division of labour is difficult to verify in contemporary records, but the family’s association with Bianchi racing bicycles lasted.'],
    ['When the large department withdrew','When Bianchi later reduced or closed its special racing production, demand for made-to-measure bicycles remained. Drali’s history says that in the 1970s Peppino built special Bianchi frames for riders outside standard dimensions. Bianchi supplied materials and name; Drali’s workshop supplied the work. They became known as Bianchi Specialissima, elaborata da Drali.'],
    ['A frame began with a body','Made to measure meant translating legs, torso, flexibility and intended use into tubes and angles. Tubes were cut and mitred to meet closely in their lugs. After brazing came alignment and clean filing. Small differences remained noticeable long after paint concealed the work.'],
    ['What the paint does not say','Was such a bicycle a Bianchi or a Drali? Both is the most useful answer. The detailed claims come chiefly from Drali’s own history and are also family and corporate memory. The craft remains visible: behind some Bianchi frames stood a file, a jig and a man in Milan whose name was not written in the largest letters.']
  ], sources:[['Drali Milano: company timeline','https://www.dralimilano.it/en/il-brand/storia-timeline/'],['Drali Milano: racing history','https://www.dralimilano.it/storia-corse/'],['Cicli Drali Milano: racing history','https://www.ciclidralimilano.it/en/storia-corse/']] },
  'da-pastaen-overtog-morgenbordet': { year:'1980s', label:'Food, training and science · Race food', title:'When pasta took over the breakfast table', intro:'Steak did not disappear in a single morning. During the 1980s, pasta, rice and sweet drinks became things teams could measure and use more systematically.', alt:'Illustration of a 1980s cycling team eating pasta, rice, bread and fruit', caption:'Period-style illustration of a team meal in the 1980s. Created for this article; not historical documentation.', sections:[
    ['Two eras on one table','Eggs and meat remain for the rider who trusts the old way. Beside them sit pasta and rice, bread, fruit and sweet tea. The soigneur fills bottles with powder. The dietary shift did not look like a locked door between past and present. It looked like a breakfast table carrying both.'],
    ['Carbohydrate became a working tool','Physiologists had studied muscle glycogen for years, but during the 1980s the thinking became more visible in team routine. Trained cyclists took part in trials of high-carbohydrate diets and drinks. The results gave coaches a language for what riders already knew in practice: empty stores make for poor racing.'],
    ['Pasta was easy to understand','Pasta became the visible symbol because it was cheap, familiar and easy to serve to many. Rice, bread, potatoes and fruit performed the same work. Protein and fat did not leave the kitchen, but steak lost its place as the obvious fuel for the stage itself.'],
    ['Some food moved into the bottle','Maltodextrin and other mixtures allowed riders to drink part of their energy. A Tour study published in 1989 recorded five riders’ diets: about 62 per cent of energy came from carbohydrate. Other work described enriched drinks supplying a substantial share of daily intake. The stomach still had a vote, and concentrated sweet mixtures could be hard to tolerate.'],
    ['From kitchen instinct to records','By the decade’s end, researchers could weigh food and calculate energy through an entire Tour. Modern plans are more precise, but their predecessor sits on this hotel table: pasta in large dishes, powder in the bottles and a notebook in which someone attempts to calculate what the rider actually eats.']
  ], sources:[['PubMed: carbohydrate drinks, cycling performance and glycogen use (1987)','https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/3821453/'],['PubMed: Food and fluid intake during a 22-day cycling race (1989)','https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2535605/'],['PubMed: Tour de France simulation study, diet manipulation (1989)','https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/2663742/'],['International Journal of Sport Nutrition: review of carbohydrate in cycling','https://doi.org/10.1123/ijsnem.2025-0239']] },
  'yvonne-reynders-kulcykel-og-verdensmesterskaber': { year:'1959–1966', label:"Riders of the period · Women's cycling", title:'When the coal had been delivered, training began', intro:"Yvonne Reynders delivered coal on a three-wheeled cargo bicycle before winning seven world titles on road and track. It built strong legs, but it did not make women's cycling a profession.", alt:'Yvonne Reynders riding at the 1967 track world championships in Amsterdam', caption:'Yvonne Reynders during the 1967 track world championships in Amsterdam. Photograph: Joop van Bilsen/Anefo, Nationaal Archief via Wikimedia Commons, CC0.', sections:[
    ['A cargo bicycle loaded with coal','The sacks had to reach the customers. Yvonne Reynders was sixteen when her father gave her a three-wheeled cargo bicycle and a round of coal deliveries. That bicycle had not been built for training zones or tidy lap times. It simply had to move a heavy load through the streets. Day after day. To my mind, that is a rather practical beginning for a world champion.'],
    ['Athletics first, then bicycle racing','Cycling was not her first sport. Reynders threw the discus, put the shot and swam. In her later account, the decisive moment came when she saw a newspaper headline announcing that women would again race bicycles in Belgium after a long pause. A customer of the family coal business found her a bicycle. The family followed in a van. She was under way.'],
    ['Four times on the road','Her first road world title came in 1959. She won again in 1961, 1963 and 1966. At Ronse in 1963, the championship ended in a sprint between sixteen riders, and Reynders beat her compatriot Rosa Sels. She could ride hard over Belgian cobbles and still had the finish to complete the job.'],
    ['Around the track as well','On the track, she won three more world titles in the individual pursuit. There was no wheel to hide behind. Two riders started on opposite sides of the track, and the clock measured the rest. The strength that had moved coal through the city now held a steady speed on banked boards. Seven world titles in all.'],
    ['A world champion without a professional wage',"The results did not make women's cycling professional. Reynders later recalled that women could race for nothing as a warm-up while male amateurs received considerably larger starting money. In winter she worked. In summer she raced. Even the rainbow jersey did not change the basic calculation very much."],
    ['Back on the bicycle','She stopped racing, then returned after several years away. In 1976 she again won the Belgian championship and took world bronze. It may be the best detail of the lot. Not because she needed another medal, but because she looked at the field and decided she could still belong there. The coal-laden cargo bicycle had long gone. The working habits remained.']
  ], sources:[['KOERS Museum: portrait of Yvonne Reynders','https://servicekoers.be/verhalen/portret-van-yvonne-reynders'],['UCI: road world champions','https://fr.uci.org/article/cyclisme-sur-route-l-histoire-et-les-records-du-maillot-arc-en-ciel/3Qvg8Z7qJQeieqj8EYNoCd'],['Cycling Weekly: How delivering coal helped Yvonne Reynders','https://www.cyclingweekly.com/news/latest-news/how-they-used-to-train-how-delivering-coal-helped-yvonne-reynders-become-a-multiple-world-champion-298257'],['Wikimedia Commons: Yvonne Reynders at the 1967 track worlds','https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:WK_Wielrennen._Achtervolging_dames_Yvonne_Reynders_(Belgi%C3%AB)_in_aktie,_Bestanddeelnr_920-6305.jpg']] },
  'tourens-taxaregning-1926': { year:'1926', label:'Odd moments from the races · Tour de France', title:'When the Tour had to pay the taxi fare', intro:'Three riders stepped out of a car in Luchon and disappeared, according to a story passed down from the 1926 Tour. The driver instead took his bill to the race officials.', alt:"Lucien Buysse and Omer Huysse on the wet Col d'Aubisque during the 1926 Tour de France", caption:'Lucien Buysse and Omer Huysse on the Col d’Aubisque during the Bayonne–Luchon stage in 1926. Photograph: Agence Meurisse/Bibliothèque nationale de France via Wikimedia Commons, public domain.', sections:[
    ['A man with a bill',"At the officials' table in Luchon, a local motorist wanted his money. The story goes that he had picked up three freezing riders in the mountains and driven them to the finish town. When the car stopped, the passengers got out and disappeared without paying. The Tour now had to decide whether the man was a rescuer, a taxi driver or an accomplice to a breach of the rules."],
    ["Into the darkness at two o'clock",'The stage from Bayonne to Luchon began at two in the morning on 6 July. More than 320 kilometres waited across the Aubisque, Tourmalet, Aspin and Peyresourde. Rain made the gravel roads heavy, and the cold took the feeling from the riders’ hands. On the bicycles of the period, a puncture or loose wheel could become far more than a brief stop.'],
    ['Buysse kept going',"Lucien Buysse rode away from the others and reached Luchon after 17 hours, 12 minutes and 4 seconds. Bartolomeo Aimo arrived almost 26 minutes later. Then the gaps grew. The Tour's official history says that 54 of 76 riders completed the stage and that several took a bus without paying. The race had simply lost track of part of its own field."],
    ['The car in the story',"The account of the three riders and the driver's demand appears in later Tour literature. The riders' names and the exact conversation at the officials' table are harder to establish. Yet the story fits the documented disorder: race cars searched for missing riders, some abandoned and found motorised transport, and the finish received people carrying very different explanations."],
    ['Rules meet mountain weather',"A ride in a car normally meant disqualification. That evening was less simple. First the organisers had to discover who was still on the mountain and who merely had to be brought to safety. According to the anecdote, the race management paid the driver from the Tour's funds. The riders were finished in the race in any case. It was not pretty, but the bill was settled."],
    ['The small aftermath','Buysse took the yellow jersey and kept it to Paris. His victory appears in the results. The driver does not. Even so, his supposed journey tells us something the times and placings cannot: when a bicycle race is sent into darkness and bad weather, the people beside the road become part of the organisation, whether the organiser planned it or not.']
  ], sources:[['Tour de France: Jules Buysse and the 1926 race','https://www.letour.fr/en/news/2018/the-achievement-of-a-lifetime-jules-buysse-ii-x'],['L’Équipe: result of Bayonne–Luchon 1926','https://www.lequipe.fr/Cyclisme-sur-route/tour-de-france/annee-1926/page-classement-individuel/10eme-etape-bayonne-luchon'],['Bill and Carol McGann: The Story of the Tour de France, volume 1','https://www.velopress.com/books/the-story-of-the-tour-de-france-volume-1/'],['Wikimedia Commons: Buysse and Huysse on the Aubisque in 1926','https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tour_de_France_cycliste_-_%C3%A9tape_Bayonne_Luchon_-_Lucien_Buysse_et_Omer_Huysse_en_t%C3%AAte_dans_le_col_d%27Aubisque_-_btv1b90256845.jpg']] },
} as const;

